**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) platform supporting multi-user operations with role hierarchy, temporary delegation, clearance-based authorization, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces security constraints such as clearance-based permissions, role delegation and revocation, and preserves safety, liveness, and integrity under concurrent operations. A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, enabling a global/system-wide operational mode and privileges during emergencies, with specific triggering, duration, and exit requirements. Emergency mode is a system-wide state: when Emergency mode is triggered, all active sessions enter Emergency mode together, and no session may remain in or independently return to Normal mode until the Emergency ends. Emergency mode can be triggered exactly once per session by exactly 2 administrators, is active for an abstract fixed duration but may be ended early by the same 2 administrators, and grants special privileges only to the triggering administrators, applied globally across all sessions. While Emergency mode is active, new triggers are blocked. Roles delegated during Emergency mode are revoked upon exit, while roles delegated prior persist as per their policy.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Only exactly 2 administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode when the system is in Normal mode; each session may only trigger Emergency mode once.
- Emergency mode is a global/system-wide state and is time-limited by an abstract fixed duration and cannot persist indefinitely; the system must ensure eventual return to Normal mode for all sessions together. Early termination is permitted by the same 2 administrators who triggered Emergency mode.
- No new Emergency mode triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.
- During Emergency mode, request processing order may be bypassed to prioritize high classification requests; this relaxation is temporary and applies system-wide.
- Users may temporarily hold predefined mutually exclusive roles only during Emergency mode.
- Only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions, and only for the duration of Emergency mode. Emergency privileges are assigned and revoked globally across all sessions.
- All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked when Emergency mode ends. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode persist until expiry or administrator revocation.
- Administrators who held emergency privileges are required to update their credentials upon exit from Emergency mode before resuming normal operations.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
Exactly 2 administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, are required to trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode globally. Each session may only trigger Emergency mode once, and no new emergency triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
For every EmergencyTrigger, the system MUST return to NormalMode for all sessions together no later than EmergencyTrigger.endedAt, and remain in NormalMode for all subsequent times. Emergency mode may end either by an abstract fixed duration (timeout) or by early termination by the same 2 administrators who triggered it.

R3: Modified Processing and Privileges During Emergency  
R3.1: During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests simultaneously, system-wide.  
R3.2: During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles (as defined in the set of mutually exclusive abstract roles).  
R3.3: During Emergency mode, only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode temporarily hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions available exclusively during Emergency mode. Emergency privileges are assigned and revoked globally across all sessions.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1: After Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked system-wide from the administrators who held them.  
R4.2: All roles that were delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked upon exit from Emergency mode. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode (during Normal mode) remain active until their expiry or until revoked by an administrator. For verification, at least one role delegation must occur during Emergency mode and during Normal mode.
R4.3: Administrators who held emergency privileges must update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.
```

### ABSTRACTION GUIDANCE:
Ask questions only when the answer affects what must be verified, not how the requirement is implemented or modeled.

Ask when:
- Behavioral ambiguity affects verification
- Cardinality affects consistency
- Temporal ordering matters
- Missing scenarios need confirmation
- A requirement may be ambiguous, inconsistent, incomplete, or logically weak.


Do not ask when:
- the issue is only an implementation or modeling choice,
- the detail can be safely abstracted,
- different answers would not change the verification structure.

### DEFAULT ASSUMPTION POLICY
If the user does not answer a clarification question, continue with the smallest reasonable assumption.

Use the weakest assumption that:
- is enough to proceed,
- preserves the abstract requirement intent,
- and adds no unnecessary constraints or behavior.

State the assumption explicitly and treat it as provisional.

