[DIAGNOSIS]:
The error message "Syntax error at line 22, column 1" indicates that Alloy encountered an unexpected token "directAssignment" at the top level of the model, right after a series of signature (sig) and abstract sig declarations. In Alloy 6, top-level relation or field declarations must either be inside a sig, pred, fun, or fact block. The statement directAssignment: User -> Role is not inside any such block, making it invalid syntax. This violates the Alloy 6 rule that only sigs, facts, predicates, functions, opens, and enums are permitted at the top level.

[FIX INTENT]:
Move the directAssignment relation declaration into an appropriate sig or declare it as a top-level sig.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Replace the line directAssignment: User -> Role with sig directAssignment { } at the top level if the intention is to model it as a set of User->Role pairs, or
- Step 2: If directAssignment is intended to be a relation, declare it as a top-level relation using the Alloy 6 syntax: 
  ```alloy
  rel directAssignment: User -> Role
  ```
  Place this line outside of any sig, after all sig and enum declarations, but before any fact, pred, or fun blocks.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix wraps the relation in a valid Alloy 6 construct (either as a sig or using the rel keyword), making it syntactically legal at the top level, and addresses the precise cause of the error (illegal top-level declaration).

[LESSON]:
Alloy 6 only allows relations to be introduced at the top-level using sig, rel, enum, fact, pred, fun, or open statements; free-standing relation declarations are not permitted.

