- What: Syntax is now OK; baseline and all incremental requirement predicates (R1, R1R2, R1R2R3, R1R2R3R4, All_Requirements) are SAT; DelegationCoverageNormal is SAT, but DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage are UNSAT; assertion failures remain for assertDelegationCoverage and assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs.
- Expected vs. Actual: Regression log expected DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage to become SAT after the prior fix, but they remain UNSAT. The assertion failures for delegation coverage and emergency privileges also persist unexpectedly.
- Likely Cause: Requirement issue (ambiguity or hidden contradiction in requirements regarding the coexistence and coverage of delegations in both modes and emergency privilege semantics); possibly a missing assumption or an overconstraint in the model enforcing stricter revocation than required.

=== REGRESSION DIAGNOSIS ===
Discrepancies: DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage predicates remain UNSAT, and counterexamples for assertDelegationCoverage and assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs persist, despite model updates intended to resolve revocation and privilege assignment semantics. This is unexpected per the regression log, which anticipated these issues would be resolved.
Analysis: The persistence of UNSAT and assertion failures, despite model and requirement adjustments (especially regarding revocation timing and the separation of delegation coverage per mode), indicates either an unresolved requirement conflict or ambiguity, or a latent modeling overconstraint. The incremental predicates (R1, R1R2, R1R2R3, R1R2R3R4) are SAT, suggesting the issue is specific to the witness/coverage properties and possibly to the strict timing or coexistence of delegation and revocation constraints at the Emergency/Normal transition.

=== OUTCOME CLASSIFICATION ===
Classification: spec_clarification: Results reveal either a requirement ambiguity or a hidden conflict concerning the coexistence of active delegations in both Emergency and Normal modes and the semantics/timing of emergency privilege assignment/revocation.
Rationale: The persistent UNSAT on DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage, together with assertion failures, despite model adjustments and passing incremental requirements, points to a requirement ambiguity or contradiction rather than a pure modeling error.

=== BLOCKING QUESTIONS ===
1. Is it permissible for different delegation instances to be active in Emergency mode and in Normal mode (i.e., must there always exist at least one delegation in each mode, but not necessarily the same delegation instance persisting across both modes), or is the requirement that at least one such instance exists for each mode, even if no delegation ever persists across the Emergency/Normal transition?
  Why needed: Clarifies whether DelegationCoverage should witness distinct delegations per mode (which appears to be unsatisfiable under strict revocation at Emergency end), or if some overlap or cross-mode persistence is required/permitted.
2. Should the requirement for emergency privileges (R3.3) allow for any delay or overlap at the transition from Emergency to Normal mode, or must privileges be revoked atomically (i.e., no state in Normal mode may have any emergency privileges assigned)?
  Why needed: Resolves possible contradiction between privilege revocation timing and the requirement to have a well-defined witness of privilege assignment and revocation, which impacts the assertion failures.
3. Is it acceptable for Emergency mode to have no delegations at all in some scenarios, or must there always be at least one delegation active during Emergency (i.e., is DelegationCoverageEmergency a strict requirement in all system executions, or is it only needed for verification/test purposes)?
  Why needed: Determines if the witness property is stronger than the intended requirement and whether the model should encode this as a soft/diagnostic property rather than a hard requirement.

=== Systematic Unsatisfiability Analysis ===
[DelegationCoverageEmergency, DelegationCoverage]

DelegationCoverageEmergency requires: 
- Existence of at least one State in EmergencyMode with some active Delegation where: 
  • d.duringEmergency = True 
  • d.revoked = False 
  • d.delegatedAt ≤ sE.t < d.expiresAt (or no expiresAt)

DelegationCoverage requires this for both Emergency and Normal modes (distinct delegations, not cross-mode persistence).

Key atomic constraints:
- All Emergency-mode delegations are revoked at Emergency/Normal transition (EmergencyDelegationAndPrivilegeRevocation fact).
- Roles delegated during Emergency must have d.duringEmergency = True.
- Delegations delegated before Emergency (d.duringEmergency = False) persist unless expired/revoked.

Blocking points:
- At Emergency/Normal transition, all Emergency-mode delegations are revoked before the first NormalMode state.
- The requirement for at least one Emergency-mode delegation to exist at some EmergencyMode state, and at least one Normal-mode delegation at some NormalMode state, is possible only if delegations are created in each mode but not required to persist across the transition.
- If the only delegation created is during Emergency and it is revoked before first Normal mode state, and no new Normal-mode delegation is created after Emergency ends, the Normal-mode witness fails.
- If only Normal-mode delegations exist, the Emergency-mode witness fails.

