=== OUTCOME CLASSIFICATION ===
Classification: spec_clarification: Results reveal a requirement ambiguity or contradiction—specifically, the model cannot witness both an active Emergency-mode delegation and a Normal-mode delegation in the same run, while also enforcing that all Emergency-mode delegations are revoked at Emergency/Normal transition and that only triggering admins ever hold emergency privileges.
Rationale: Persistent unsatisfiability and new assertion failures indicate that the requirements regarding delegation coverage, emergency privilege assignment, and revocation are either incomplete, mutually exclusive, or ambiguous, requiring user clarification before the model can be corrected.

=== BLOCKING QUESTIONS ===
1. Is it required that, in every possible scenario, both an Emergency-mode delegation (active during Emergency) and a Normal-mode delegation (active during Normal) must exist within the same system execution, even though all Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked before Normal mode resumes?
  Why needed: The current requirements and coverage predicates cannot be jointly satisfied—if all Emergency-mode delegations are revoked at transition, then it may be impossible to witness both types of active delegation in the same instance unless cross-mode overlap is allowed or the coverage witness is relaxed.
2. Should the DelegationCoverage witness predicate only require that such delegations are possible in some scenario (not necessarily within the same execution), or is it intended to require both types to be present in every complete system execution?
  Why needed: This determines whether the model must be able to witness both delegation types simultaneously (which appears impossible under current revocation rules), or if it is sufficient to verify each scenario in isolation.
3. Is it necessary that, at every point during Emergency mode, only the triggering administrators have emergency privileges, or is there any allowance for privilege handoff, delayed revocation, or privilege assignment errors?
  Why needed: The persistent counterexample to assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs suggests that either the model fails to enforce this, or the requirement is too strict or ambiguous regarding possible privilege transitions at Emergency end.
4. Should Emergency-mode delegations be allowed to persist into the first Normal state (i.e., revocation may occur after the Emergency/Normal transition), or must they be revoked strictly before or at the transition instant?
  Why needed: This affects whether the assertion/fact requiring immediate revocation at transition is too strong, and whether a different model of revocation timing is needed.

=== Systematic Unsatisfiability Analysis ===
DelegationCoverageEmergency (and DelegationCoverage) remain persistently UNSAT.
1. Decompose: DelegationCoverageEmergency requires at least one Emergency-mode delegation to be active (not revoked, valid time window) during Emergency mode. DelegationCoverage requires both Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegations to be actively present (distinct, not cross-mode).
2. Likely Blocker: The EmergencyDelegationAndPrivilegeRevocation and related facts/requirements enforce that all Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked at or before the Emergency/Normal transition, so no Emergency-mode delegation can persist into Normal mode. If the system execution requires both to be witnessed in the same run, and if sequences cannot be constructed to allow both, the predicate is UNSAT.
3. Mapping: The requirement to witness both types of delegation conflicts with the requirement that all Emergency-mode delegations are revoked before Normal mode resumes, unless there is a period of overlap or unless the coverage witness is only intended to require possibility, not coexistence.
